    "description": "Built before Suez Canal",
    "notes": "The trickiest route in the set: the 1858 original is largely gone and undocumented; the mapped points follow the modern Cairo Ain Shams–Suez desert line. Verify everything here against maps.  [description] Opened 1858 as the first Mediterranean–Red Sea land link (pre-dating the 1869 Canal); the original desert alignment was later altered/partly abandoned, so the historic corridor is uncertain.  [route] TWO DISTINCT LINES share these endpoints: (1) the ORIGINAL 1858 desert line — historic alignment and stations essentially UNDOCUMENTED in consulted sources, a genuine research gap needing historical maps; and (2) the MODERN Cairo Ain Shams–Suez desert line, whose stations per Wikipedia are: Cairo Ain Shams, Abur, Kilo 3 / Kilo 11.5 / Kilo 13 / Kilo 82.825 (distance-marker halts), Tabiyet el-Agrud, Wadi el-Sil, Rabiki, Shuruq, Suez. Only the placeable modern stations are mapped here (Ain Shams, Shuruq, Tabiyet el-Agrud); the rest await coordinates. This route is LOW confidence.",
    "history": "Opened 1858 as the first Mediterranean–Red Sea land link, pre-dating the 1869 Suez Canal. In the early period it reportedly carried goods traffic; a later/modern 'desert railway' on this corridor (Cairo Ain Shams–Suez) was worked by streamlined Ganz (Budapest) diesel rail-cars in the mid-1930s, described in period sources as among the first air-conditioned rail-cars in the world.",
